# ALL.mask -- line masks for gate_cases_snobol4 (FORMAT: entry<TAB>python-regex<TAB>reason), read by corpus_suite_harness.py read_mask_sidecar().
test_gate_collect_reports_free_storage_and_fails_when_short__f	^RET=[0-9]+$	COLLECT() answers the free bytes of the heap window, a figure of the implementation: SCRIP reports 1043040 at the shipped 1 MB window and 2091616 and 4188768 at 2 and 4 MB (SCRIP_HEAP_KB), SPITBOL its own 129481 (the coo 2026-11-13, on junior_11 and senior_09 measurements); the line stays and its figure is masked, as thread/gc collection count is
*	^memory used \(bytes\)  ([0-9]+|-)$	SPITBOL's own allocator arithmetic: a quantity of the engine's heap bookkeeping, not of the program's data. Deterministic per program (five identical runs; unmoved by -s2m/-s8m/-m2m) and therefore earned under CEO-420 clause (b), not clause (a): two programs with byte-identical output read 11320 and 16456. No correct implementation can produce SPITBOL's figure by computing anything, only by transcribing it. CEO-1344: SCRIP's rendered fatal block carries a dash where it carries no figure, so the placeholder lands on both sides.
*	^memory left \(bytes\)  ([0-9]+|-)$	The same allocator arithmetic from the other side, and the same demonstration: 1037248 vs 1032112 for two programs whose output is byte-identical. Masked with its twin or not at all -- masking one and grading the other would grade the arena size, which is neither engine's answer to anything the program asked. CEO-1344: the rendered block's dash is admitted.
*	^stmts executed       ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344 (a SPITBOL fatal is gradable, the coo 2026-09-28): SPITBOL's own statement counter, printed in its fatal block -- oracle-internal until SCRIP reports its own count; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
*	^execution time msec  ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344: SPITBOL's own clock reading in its fatal block, a duration and never a program's answer; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
*	^REGENERATIONS        ([0-9]+|-)$	CEO-1344: SPITBOL's own collector count in its fatal block, engine bookkeeping and not the program's data; SCRIP's rendered block carries a dash.
